PART G -- POINT 5: IS THE FAVOURABLE STRUCTURE REAL?
====================================================================================================
  [ok] G1  *** IT IS REAL, AND IT IS WORTH 2.5e+11x. a0(z=1090)/a0(0) = 0.0060 puts the MOND phantom OFF at recombination, so the sector must cluster THEN; c_s^2 ~ a^(3s) makes it coldest THEN.  Same direction, and it converts stage 9's 595 c^2 (superluminal) into 2.4e-09 c^2 ***
  [ok] G2  *** AND IT DOES NOT CHANGE THE VERDICT, which is the honest half.  Both surviving constraints -- 'smooth in a galaxy' and 'clustered at 1-10 Mpc' -- are evaluated at THE SAME EPOCH, z ~ 0-3.  A sound speed is scale-free, so a time-dependence cannot separate two requirements that differ in SCALE, not in TIME.  The rise relieves the constraint that was never binding ***   PRECISE ATTRIBUTION, because D2 showed the s-cancellation is NOT exact: at z = 0 ALONE the rise does buy enough at k = 0.2 h/Mpc (ratio 0.37 at s = 8).  What closes it is C1e -- a steeply rising c_s^2 is cold exactly while the galaxies are being assembled, and (1+z)^(3s) takes back everything the rise bought, with interest
  [ok] G3  WHAT I COULD NOT DETERMINE: (i) whether MOND-boosted baryons alone can rebuild P(k) at k ~ 0.2-1 h/Mpc once the sector is smoothed -- that is a full MOND-cosmology N-body question and this run does not answer it, so the k = 1 h/Mpc leg is priced against a LCDM-calibrated yardstick and inherits that assumption; (ii) whether a SECOND field carrying the pressure evades the collision, since the degeneracy in D2 is a property of one barotropic fluid; (iii) the nonlinear response of a Gamma < 1 fluid in a collapsing region, which is treated here only in hydrostatic equilibrium

